  "draftIsFinal": false,
  "summary": "I wrote src/main.ts and it passes typecheck. I couldn't build or run it in a browser, so none of the visuals or the drag behaviour have been checked on screen.\n\n- **Cube:** one `primitives.box` with `material.pbr` (bronze colour, metalness 0.9, roughness 0.38). The cube is tilted and has a `turntable` animation, so it rotates by itself.\n- **Lighting:** a studio environment for reflections (its background is off), plus a warm key, a blue fill and a rim directional light. A little ambient light is added. The background is dark grey `#2a2a2a`.\n- **Orbit:** I didn't use `interactions.orbit()`. I found no code that reads it at runtime, so it only declares intent. Instead the canvas has pointer-drag handlers that change yaw and pitch (pitch clamped to ±85°). An `app.onFrame` callback writes the orbit position into the scene's camera object every frame, so dragging works while the cube spins. This relies on the renderer reading that camera object each frame. The engine's own camera extension depends on the same behaviour, but I didn't confirm it by running.\n- **Framing:** the camera is a perspective camera with a 40° field of view, 5.2 units from the cube, aimed at the origin, so the whole cube is centred.\n- **Contract:** it draws into the existing `#app` canvas, which `createAuraApp` leaves at its current size. `window.__EVAL_READY__` is set after the renderer has mounted and two animation frames have passed.\n\nI used `box` because the task asks for a cube. The docs say not to build hero objects from primitives, but that applies to named objects like characters and products.\n\n",
  "finalTypecheckPass": true,
